---
/*
 * The README's platform table, reproduced word for word.
 *
 * The Windows row is the reason this component exists. It says the code has
 * never run on Windows and should not be treated as working, and it is stated
 * on the landing page at full strength — not softened, not folded into a
 * disclosure, not moved to a footnote. A project willing to publish its own
 * limitation is making the strongest available claim about everything else it
 * says, and hiding this row would leave the site less truthful than the README
 * it is built from.
 *
 * Written out rather than generated from data: each row carries its own
 * emphasis and code spans, and the wording has to be diffable against the
 * README by eye.
 */
---

<div class="platform scroll-x" tabindex="0">
  <table>
    <caption class="sr-only">gitwho platform support, stated per platform</caption>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">macOS</th>
        <td>verified — this is where it runs every day</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Linux</th>
        <td>
          the test suite passes on x86-64 in CI. On Debian/aarch64 the whole <code>init</code> flow
          has also been driven by hand: store modes, identity, the credential helper, a shim
          executed for real, and the <code>.bashrc</code> PATH line
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Windows</th>
        <td>
          the <code>%APPDATA%</code> path, the <code>.cmd</code> shim and the <code>PATHEXT</code>
          lookup are unit-tested as pure functions <strong
            >from macOS, and have never run on Windows</strong
          >. Do not treat them as working
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<style>
  /*
   * The global `overflow-x` rule is scoped to `.prose table`, and this table
   * sits outside the reading column so its second cell has room. It therefore
   * carries its own scroller — `.scroll-x` in global.css — rather than being
   * allowed to widen the page on a phone.
   */
  .platform {
    margin-top: var(--s5);
    /* Not the full 68rem shell: the Linux row would run past a readable line
       length long before the page ran out of room. */
    max-width: 48rem;
  }

  .platform table {
    width: 100%;
  }

  /*
   * A platform name is a proper noun, not something you type, so it stays in
   * the sans face and at full ink — the site's mono-for-typeable rule would
   * otherwise dim exactly the word the row is about.
   */
  .platform th {
    width: 1%;
    padding-inline-end: var(--s5);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--t-md);
    font-weight: var(--w-semi);
    letter-spacing: normal;
    white-space: nowrap;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .platform td {
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  /* The one sentence on this page that must not be skimmed past. */
  .platform strong {
    font-weight: var(--w-semi);
    color: var(--text);
  }
</style>
